# XCU dd
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Inputs are regular files (reads return whole blocks) except where a test
# is about pipes. Tests run with LC_ALL=C. The statistics lines on standard
# error are matched only in the formats the page fixes for the POSIX
# locale; other diagnostics are never compared. Block counts of skipped
# blocks, count=0, cbs missing or zero, repeated non-conv operands and the
# effect of bad size expressions are unspecified and not tested.

assertion  XCU.dd.001
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       if=file and of=file copy the input file to the output file byte
           for byte (including NUL and high bytes) with the default block
           sizes, and an existing output file longer than the input is
           truncated to the copied data when neither seek nor notrunc is given.
test       t/001-if-of.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.002
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDIN
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       Without if= the input is standard input and without of= the output
           is standard output, and nothing but the copied data goes to
           standard output.
test       t/002-stdin-stdout.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.003
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDERR
anchor     utilities/dd.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       On success dd exits with status zero and writes on standard error,
           in the POSIX locale, one line N+M records in and one line N+M
           records out, where N counts whole blocks and M partial blocks,
           with the default block size of 512 bytes.
test       t/003-statistics.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.004
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       ibs= and obs= set the input and output block sizes separately:
           input is read in ibs blocks, collected and written in full obs
           blocks with a shorter last block, and the record counts follow
           those sizes.
test       t/004-ibs-obs.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.005
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       bs= sets both block sizes and supersedes ibs= and obs= given
           before it.
test       t/005-bs.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.006
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       Sizes may be written as a decimal number, with the suffix k (times
           1024) or b (times 512), or as two or more such numbers joined by
           x giving their product.
test       t/006-size-expressions.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.007
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       count=n copies only n input blocks.
test       t/007-count.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.008
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDIN
class      required
since      1992
says       skip=n skips n input blocks of the input block size before
           copying, for a file named by if= and for a regular file on standard
           input, and combines with count=.
test       t/008-skip.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.009
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       On a non-seekable input (a pipe) skip=n reads and discards n
           blocks and copies the rest.
test       t/009-skip-pipe.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.010
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       seek=n skips n output blocks of the output block size from the
           start of the output file; without notrunc the blocks skipped over
           are kept and nothing after the written data is kept, so a longer
           old file is shortened.
test       t/010-seek.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.011
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       With an empty input and seek beyond the old end of the output file
           (or a missing output file) the output size becomes the seek
           offset; with an empty input and seek short of the old end the file
           is shortened to the seek offset.
test       t/011-seek-empty.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.012
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=notrunc leaves the old contents of the output file that dd
           does not write, with or without seek=.
test       t/012-notrunc.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.013
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=lcase maps uppercase letters to lowercase and conv=ucase maps
           lowercase to uppercase in the POSIX locale, leaving every other
           byte unchanged, independently of the input block boundaries.
test       t/013-case.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.014
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=swab swaps every pair of input bytes and leaves a final odd
           byte of the input in place.
test       t/014-swab.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.015
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=sync pads a short input block with NUL bytes up to the input
           block size, the padding is processed and written like input, and
           with block or unblock the padding is spaces instead; a short block
           is counted as a partial input block.
test       t/015-sync.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.016
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDERR
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=block with cbs=n turns each newline-terminated or EOF-
           terminated record into a fixed record of n bytes: the newline is
           dropped, short records are padded with spaces, long ones are
           truncated, independently of the input block size; the number of
           truncated records is reported on standard error in the form N
           truncated record or N truncated records.
test       t/016-block.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.017
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       conv=unblock with cbs=n takes n bytes at a time (the rest of the
           input if fewer), deletes trailing spaces and appends a newline,
           independently of the input block size.
test       t/017-unblock.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.018
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       Several conversions can be given as a comma-separated list in one
           conv= operand or in several conv= operands, and all apply.
test       t/018-conv-lists.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.019
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#STDERR
class      required
since      1992
says       With bs= and only the conversions noerror, notrunc or sync, dd
           copies the input unchanged (here without error) and the counts
           reflect the block size; notrunc keeps the tail of a longer old
           output file.
test       t/019-bs-noerror-notrunc.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.020
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       iflags=fullblock makes dd read until a full input block (or end of
           file) is obtained, so a slow pipe giving short reads still yields
           full blocks, and count= then counts full blocks.
test       t/020-fullblock.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.021
anchor     utilities/dd.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
class      required
since      1992
says       When the input file cannot be opened or the output file cannot be
           created, dd writes a diagnostic message on standard error and
           exits with a status above zero.
test       t/021-errors.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.022
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       conv=ebcdic with cbs behaves as block followed by conversion of
           the bytes to EBCDIC by the standard table, and conv=ibm does so
           with the IBM table, whose entries for the characters ^ and ~
           differ from the standard table.
test       t/022-ebcdic-ibm.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.023
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       conv=ascii with cbs behaves as unblock after converting the bytes
           from EBCDIC to ASCII by the inverse of the standard table.
test       t/023-ascii.sh

assertion  XCU.dd.024
anchor     utilities/dd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/dd.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
class      untestable
since      1992
says       With conv=noerror an input error writes a diagnostic and the block
           counts and the copy goes on (the block omitted, or replaced by NUL
           bytes with sync); without noerror the partial output block is
           written and the copy stops.
reason     A read error on the input cannot be produced portably from
           standard utilities and a regular file.

assertion  XCU.dd.025
anchor     utilities/dd.html#ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
class      untestable
since      2001
says       On SIGINT dd stops, writes status information to standard error and
           terminates as if by the default action; other signals take their
           default action except that implementation-defined non-job-control
           signals may only report status.
reason     Delivering a signal at a chosen point of a copy is not portably
           possible from scripts without timing assumptions.
